Yes — this is one of the **most important Spring transaction interview topics**.

The key point is:

> **Two methods having `@Transactional` does NOT automatically mean two database transactions are created.**

Whether there is **one transaction or multiple transactions** depends mainly on:

1. Transaction propagation (`REQUIRED`, `REQUIRES_NEW`, etc.)
2. Whether the method call goes through the **Spring proxy**
3. Whether the second method is called from the same bean (`this.method2()`) or another Spring bean.

---

# 1. Simple case — both use `@Transactional`

Suppose we have:

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {

    @Transactional
    public void method1() {

        System.out.println("Method 1");

        method2();
    }

    @Transactional
    public void method2() {

        System.out.println("Method 2");
    }
}
```

And:

```java
orderService.method1();
```

### What happens?

By default:

```java
@Transactional
```

uses:

```java
Propagation.REQUIRED
```

So conceptually:

```text
Caller
   |
   v
Spring Proxy
   |
   |-- BEGIN TRANSACTION
   |
   v
method1()
   |
   v
method2()
   |
   |-- same transaction
   |
   v
method1() returns
   |
   |-- COMMIT TRANSACTION
```

There is **ONE physical transaction**.

Not two.

---

# 2. Why is there only one transaction?

Because the default propagation is:

```java
Propagation.REQUIRED
```

Its meaning is:

> "If a transaction already exists, use it. Otherwise create a new one."

So when:

```java
method1()
```

starts:

```text
No transaction exists
        ↓
method1() @Transactional
        ↓
BEGIN TX-1
```

Now when `method2()` executes:

```text
method2() @Transactional
        ↓
Does transaction already exist?
        ↓
YES → TX-1
        ↓
Use TX-1
```

Therefore:

```text
method1() → TX-1
method2() → TX-1
```

---

# 3. But there is an important catch: self-invocation

The previous example has another important concept.

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {

    @Transactional
    public void method1() {
        method2();
    }

    @Transactional
    public void method2() {
    }
}
```

This:

```java
method2();
```

is actually:

```java
this.method2();
```

The call does **not go through the Spring proxy**.

This is called:

> **Self-invocation**

And Spring's proxy-based transaction management cannot intercept that second call.

---

# 4. Does that mean method2 has no transaction?

Not exactly.

If `method1()` was called from outside:

```java
orderService.method1();
```

then Spring proxy intercepts `method1()`:

```text
Caller
   |
   v
Spring Proxy
   |
   | @Transactional
   | BEGIN TX-1
   |
   v
method1()
   |
   | this.method2()
   |
   v
method2()
```

Although `method2()`'s own `@Transactional` annotation is **not intercepted**, `method2()` is still executing **inside TX-1**.

Therefore:

```text
method1() → TX-1
method2() → TX-1
```

So you still have **one transaction**.

But the important distinction is:

> `method2()`'s `@Transactional` annotation is not being applied through the proxy.

---

# 5. Example where both methods are in different beans

Now consider:

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {

    private final PaymentService paymentService;

    public OrderService(PaymentService paymentService) {
        this.paymentService = paymentService;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void method1() {

        System.out.println("Method 1");

        paymentService.method2();
    }
}
```

And:

```java
@Service
public class PaymentService {

    @Transactional
    public void method2() {

        System.out.println("Method 2");
    }
}
```

Call:

```java
orderService.method1();
```

Now:

```text
Caller
   |
   v
OrderService Proxy
   |
   | BEGIN TX-1
   |
   v
OrderService.method1()
   |
   v
PaymentService Proxy
   |
   | @Transactional(REQUIRED)
   |
   | Existing TX-1?
   | YES
   |
   v
PaymentService.method2()
   |
   | uses TX-1
   |
   v
method1 returns
   |
   | COMMIT TX-1
```

Again:

**ONE transaction.**

