# 6. The most important propagation types

This is where things become interesting.

Spring supports several transaction propagation modes.

The most important ones for interviews are:

| Propagation     | Existing TX? | Behavior                      |
| --------------- | ------------ | ----------------------------- |
| `REQUIRED`      | Yes          | Join existing                 |
| `REQUIRED`      | No           | Create new                    |
| `REQUIRES_NEW`  | Yes          | Suspend existing + create new |
| `REQUIRES_NEW`  | No           | Create new                    |
| `NESTED`        | Yes          | Nested transaction/savepoint  |
| `NESTED`        | No           | Usually behaves like REQUIRED |
| `SUPPORTS`      | Yes          | Join                          |
| `SUPPORTS`      | No           | Run without transaction       |
| `NOT_SUPPORTED` | Yes          | Suspend transaction           |
| `NOT_SUPPORTED` | No           | Run without transaction       |
| `MANDATORY`     | Yes          | Join                          |
| `MANDATORY`     | No           | Exception                     |
| `NEVER`         | Yes          | Exception                     |
| `NEVER`         | No           | Run without transaction       |

For your question, the big three are:

```text
REQUIRED
REQUIRES_NEW
NESTED
```

---

# 7. Case 1 — `REQUIRED` + `REQUIRED`

```java
@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRED)
public void method1() {

    method2();
}

@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRED)
public void method2() {
}
```

Assuming proxy interception is involved appropriately:

```text
method1
   |
   | BEGIN TX-1
   |
   v
method2
   |
   | Existing TX-1
   | JOIN
   |
   v
method1
   |
   | COMMIT TX-1
```

### Result

```text
1 physical transaction
```

---

# 8. Case 2 — `REQUIRED` + `REQUIRES_NEW`

Now:

```java
@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRED)
public void method1() {

    method2();
}

@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
public void method2() {
}
```

Assume `method2()` is called through another Spring bean/proxy.

Now things change.

```text
Caller
  |
  v
method1()
  |
  | BEGIN TX-1
  |
  v
method2()
  |
  | REQUIRES_NEW
  |
  | Suspend TX-1
  |
  | BEGIN TX-2
  |
  v
method2()
  |
  | COMMIT TX-2
  |
  | Resume TX-1
  |
  v
method1()
  |
  | COMMIT TX-1
```

Now there are:

```text
TX-1
TX-2
```

### Two separate physical transactions.

---

# 9. Very important: `REQUIRES_NEW` does not mean nested transaction

This is a common interview mistake.

Suppose:

```text
TX-1
  |
  | method1
  |
  +---- TX-2
          |
          | method2
```

You might think:

> TX-2 is inside TX-1.

Logically yes, but from the database transaction perspective:

**TX-2 is independent.**

Spring does:

```text
Suspend TX-1
      ↓
Start TX-2
      ↓
Commit/Rollback TX-2
      ↓
Resume TX-1
```

Therefore TX-2 can commit even if TX-1 subsequently rolls back.

---

# 10. Example of `REQUIRES_NEW`

Imagine:

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {

    private final AuditService auditService;

    @Transactional
    public void createOrder() {

        orderRepository.save(order);

        auditService.saveAudit();

        throw new RuntimeException("Order failed");
    }
}
```

Audit:

```java
@Service
public class AuditService {

    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
    public void saveAudit() {

        auditRepository.save(audit);
    }
}
```

Flow:

```text
createOrder()
     |
     | TX-1
     |
     | save order
     |
     v
saveAudit()
     |
     | suspend TX-1
     |
     | TX-2
     |
     | save audit
     |
     | COMMIT TX-2
     |
     | resume TX-1
     |
     v
throw RuntimeException
     |
     | ROLLBACK TX-1
```

Final database state:

```text
Order → ROLLBACK ❌

Audit → COMMITTED ✅
```

This is one of the main reasons people use:

```java
REQUIRES_NEW
```

for independent audit/logging operations.

---

# 11. Case 3 — `NESTED`

Now:

```java
@Transactional
public void method1() {

    method2();
}

@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.NESTED)
public void method2() {
}
```

With a transaction-capable resource such as JDBC where nested transaction support is available, Spring can use a **savepoint**.

Conceptually:

```text
TX-1
 |
 | method1
 |
 | SAVEPOINT
 |
 | method2
 |
 | rollback to SAVEPOINT
 |
 | continue TX-1
 |
 | COMMIT TX-1
```

So:

```text
NESTED ≠ REQUIRES_NEW
```

### `REQUIRES_NEW`

```text
TX-1 suspended
TX-2 starts
```

### `NESTED`

```text
TX-1
 |
 +-- SAVEPOINT
 |
 +-- nested work
 |
 +-- rollback to savepoint
 |
 +-- continue TX-1
```

This distinction is extremely important.

---

# 12. What happens when method2 throws an exception?

Consider:

```java
@Transactional
public void method1() {

    method2();
}

@Transactional
public void method2() {

    throw new RuntimeException("Something failed");
}
```

Because both use:

```text
REQUIRED
```

they share:

```text
TX-1
```

So:

```text
method1()
   |
   | TX-1
   |
   v
method2()
   |
   | exception
   |
   v
rollback TX-1
```

The entire transaction rolls back.

---

# 13. What if method1 catches method2's exception?

This is more interesting.

```java
@Transactional
public void method1() {

    try {
        method2();
    } catch (Exception e) {
        System.out.println("Exception handled");
    }

    orderRepository.save(order);
}
```

And:

```java
@Transactional
public void method2() {

    paymentRepository.save(payment);

    throw new RuntimeException("Payment failed");
}
```

Many developers incorrectly think:

> "I caught the exception, so the transaction will commit."

Not necessarily.

Because `method2()` can mark the transaction as:

```text
rollback-only
```

Then method1 continues:

```text
TX-1
 |
 | method2()
 |
 | exception
 |
 | TX marked rollback-only
 |
 | exception caught
 |
 | method1 continues
 |
 | method1 returns
 |
 | COMMIT attempted
 |
 | Spring sees rollback-only
 |
 | ROLLBACK
```

You may get:

```text
UnexpectedRollbackException
```

This is a very important Spring interview scenario.

---

# 14. Why does `UnexpectedRollbackException` happen?

Example:

```java
@Transactional
public void method1() {

    try {
        method2();
    } catch (RuntimeException e) {
        System.out.println("Caught");
    }

    repository.save(data);
}
```

```java
@Transactional
public void method2() {

    repository.save(data2);

    throw new RuntimeException();
}
```

Both:

```text
method1 → REQUIRED
method2 → REQUIRED
```

Therefore:

```text
method1
  |
  TX-1
  |
  method2
  |
  exception
  |
  TX-1 marked rollback-only
```

Even though the exception is caught:

```text
TX-1 = rollback-only
```

At the end:

```text
ROLLBACK
```

Potentially:

```text
UnexpectedRollbackException
```

---

# 15. What if method2 uses `REQUIRES_NEW`?

Now:

```java
@Transactional
public void method1() {

    try {
        method2();
    } catch (Exception e) {
        System.out.println("Caught");
    }

    orderRepository.save(order);
}
```

And:

```java
@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
public void method2() {

    paymentRepository.save(payment);

    throw new RuntimeException();
}
```

Flow:

```text
TX-1
 |
 | method1
 |
 | suspend TX-1
 |
 +---- TX-2
       |
       | method2
       |
       | exception
       |
       | ROLLBACK TX-2
       |
       +---->
 |
 | resume TX-1
 |
 | catch exception
 |
 | save order
 |
 | COMMIT TX-1
```

Final result:

```text
method2 data → ROLLBACK
method1 data → COMMIT
```

assuming the exception is handled and nothing else causes TX-1 to roll back.

