# 28. Transaction boundary vs method boundary

This is the key mental model.

Don't think:

```text
@Transactional method
       =
one transaction
```

Instead think:

```text
@Transactional method
       ↓
transaction interceptor
       ↓
propagation decision
       ↓
transaction boundary
```

Multiple methods can belong to the **same transaction**.

For example:

```text
TX-1
 |
 +-- method1()
 |
 +-- method2()
 |
 +-- repository.save()
 |
 +-- repository.update()
 |
 +-- method3()
 |
 +-- repository.delete()
 |
 +-- COMMIT
```

There can be 10 transactional methods participating in:

```text
ONE transaction
```

---

# 29. Interview answer you can memorize

If interviewer asks:

> **"If method A and method B both have `@Transactional` and A calls B, how many transactions are created?"**

Say:

> By default, `@Transactional` uses `Propagation.REQUIRED`. If method A starts a transaction and then calls method B, B will join the existing transaction rather than creating another transaction. Therefore, normally there is one physical transaction shared by both methods.
>
> However, if B uses `Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW` and the call goes through the Spring proxy, Spring suspends A's transaction and creates a new transaction for B. After B completes, the original transaction is resumed.
>
> Also, self-invocation is important. If A and B are in the same Spring bean and A calls B using `this.methodB()`/direct method invocation, B's `@Transactional` annotation is not intercepted by the Spring proxy. Therefore propagation settings such as `REQUIRES_NEW` on B won't take effect.

